WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:03.830
So let's begin with the AI controller.

2
00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.610
A controller in Unreal Engine 4 is a non-physical actor

3
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.609
that can possess a pawn or derived actor

4
00:00:08.609 --> 00:00:10.439
such as the character.

5
00:00:10.439 --> 00:00:12.179
Built already into Unreal Engine 4,

6
00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:14.060
we have several derived types of controllers

7
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.059
such as the one that we're discussing right now.

8
00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:17.660
But it's worth mentioning the player control

9
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.480
at the very least.

10
00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.219
The player controller can be best described

11
00:00:21.219 --> 00:00:22.620
as the will of the player.

12
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.879
So we can surmise that the AI controller

13
00:00:24.879 --> 00:00:28.839
is the simulated will of an AI agent.

14
00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:30.699
All controllers, no matter what type,

15
00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:34.270
share some common functionality and traits.

16
00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.990
The first is that the control of a pawn

17
00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:37.490
is done with the possess function

18
00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.030
and it's relinquished with the calling

19
00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.479
of the unpossessed function.

20
00:00:41.479 --> 00:00:43.740
Controllers also have a one-to-one relationship

21
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.859
with a pawn, meaning a single pawn

22
00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:47.060
can only have one controller,

23
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:50.579
which works perfectly for most AI.

24
00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:52.960
Where the AI controller differs from the base controller

25
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.799
or any derived player controller,

26
00:00:54.799 --> 00:00:57.299
is that it's exclusively created on the server

27
00:00:57.299 --> 00:00:59.219
and it's never replicated to any clients

28
00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:01.030
in a networked game.

29
00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.070
This is important as AI must be authoritative

30
00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:06.969
and in networked games, the server is that source of truth.

31
00:01:06.969 --> 00:01:09.189
The AI controller houses four main components

32
00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:11.969
that allow AI agents to function in our games.

33
00:01:11.969 --> 00:01:14.569
The components being the behavior tree component

34
00:01:14.569 --> 00:01:17.629
and this can be best described as the brain of the AI.

35
00:01:17.629 --> 00:01:18.890
The blackboard component,

36
00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.909
best described as the memory of the AI.

37
00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:24.209
The perception component, and as the name suggests,

38
00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:26.670
this derives what senses the AI has.

39
00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.230
Followed by the path following component

40
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.030
and again, pretty self-explanatory.

41
00:01:30.069 --> 00:01:33.599
This allows the AI to navigate the level.

42
00:01:33.599 --> 00:01:35.719
Previously, triggering AI logic used to be done

43
00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:37.519
either through Blueprint or through code

44
00:01:37.519 --> 00:01:40.700
by starting the behavior tree when we possess the pawn.

45
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.840
However, the AI controller now exposes control

46
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.000
in Blueprint and Native C++ to allow the AI logic

47
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.250
to begin immediately when possessing the pawn.

48
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:50.750
The AI controller also implements

49
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.920
the generic team agent interface.

50
00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.519
This interface implements functionality

51
00:01:55.519 --> 00:01:57.760
that allows for querying what team and AI agent

52
00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.920
may be on, as well as what their attitude

53
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.299
towards another actor might be.

54
00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:05.959
Unreal implements a generic team identification system

55
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.819
straight out of the box, which is flexible

56
00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:09.379
and completely up to the developer

57
00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:13.020
to implement in whatever way they see fit.

58
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.979
Lastly, the AI controller provides programs

59
00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:16.819
with a handy entry function to determine

60
00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:20.659
how our AI agent selects the target that they focus on.

61
00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:23.020
The actor's perception updated function

62
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:24.180
sends through an array of actors

63
00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.900
they have perceived recently.

64
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.219
Once again, the implementation of this is left empty

65
00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:30.780
to allow each developer to implement

66
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.759
their own target selection process.
